WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:28.900
A practical ISO/IEC 42001 implementation moves through a cycle: understand the standard, establish sponsorship, define context and interested parties, define scope, build the AI inventory, establish policy, roles, and objectives, assess risks and impacts, select controls, complete the Statement of Applicability, operate lifecycle and supplier governance, monitor evidence, audit and review, correct, and improve.

2
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:31.260
Phase one is direction.

3
00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:46.560
Confirm executive sponsorship, identify implementation leadership, obtain the authorized standard, understand organizational context, identify interested parties, define preliminary scope, and map existing management systems.

4
00:00:46.660 --> 00:00:48.580
Phase two is visibility.

5
00:00:48.680 --> 00:01:00.520
Create the AI inventory, identify owners, record intended purposes, identify suppliers and dependencies, classify systems for assessment priority, and locate unapproved or shadow AI use.

6
00:01:00.620 --> 00:01:06.300
These early phases prevent teams from designing controls for an AI estate they do not understand.

7
00:01:06.580 --> 00:01:08.740
Phase three designs the AIMS.

8
00:01:08.840 --> 00:01:22.920
Approve the AI policy, define roles and decision rights, establish objectives, set risk criteria, define risk and impact processes, plan communication, control documented information, and establish competence requirements.

9
00:01:23.020 --> 00:01:25.580
Phase four assesses and treats.

10
00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:39.360
Perform risk assessments, conduct impact assessments, select treatments, choose relevant Annex A reference controls and additional controls, complete the Statement of Applicability, assign action owners, and approve residual risks through an authorized process.

11
00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.520
Phase five operates the system.

12
00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:54.220
Integrate controls with lifecycle processes, govern data, manage suppliers, monitor AI systems and controls, handle incidents and exceptions, control changes, and preserve evidence.

13
00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.040
Phase six evaluates and improves.

14
00:01:57.140 --> 00:02:13.460
Measure objectives, conduct internal audit, perform management review, correct nonconformities, verify corrective-action effectiveness, update risks, impacts, controls, and scope, and prepare for certification if the organization wants or needs it.

15
00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:17.400
Evidence should be produced by normal work, not manufactured at the end.

16
00:02:17.680 --> 00:02:20.320
The main distinctions should remain visible.

17
00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:23.140
An AIMS is not one AI system.

18
00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:26.440
Governance is not the same as technical implementation.

19
00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:30.220
Risk assessment and impact assessment answer different questions.

20
00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.040
Control design is not proof of effectiveness.

21
00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:35.620
Policy is not procedure.

22
00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:38.280
Responsibility is not accountability.

23
00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:42.140
Annex A is a reference set, not a universal checklist.

24
00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:46.640
Internal audit differs from technical testing and certification audit.

25
00:02:46.740 --> 00:02:50.180
Certification is not a guarantee of safe AI.

26
00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:58.440
The implementation is strongest when system owners, risk owners, suppliers, auditors, and executives all know their part.

27
00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:06.140
ISO/IEC 42001 becomes effective when AI governance is part of ordinary organizational decision-making.

28
00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:15.600
The roadmap is complete only when governance is routine enough that new AI ideas, supplier changes, incidents, and retirement decisions naturally enter the AIMS.

29
00:03:15.700 --> 00:03:21.860
The result is a management system that changes with the organization instead of freezing after initial approval.
